q(xx−1∣xt,x0)=Cat(xt−1;p=xtQt⊤⊙x0Qt−1x0Q¯txt⊤) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} q\left(x_{x-1} \mid x_{t}, x_{0}\right)=\operatorname{Cat}\left(x_{t-1} ; p=\frac{x_{t} Q_{t}^{\top} \odot x_{0} Q_{t-1}}{x_{0} \bar{Q}_{t} x_{t}^{\top}}\right) \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5)
pθ(xt−1∣xt)∝∑x~0q(xt−1,xt∣x~0)p~θ(x~0∣xt) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{\theta}\left(x_{t-1} \mid x_{t}\right) \propto \sum_{\tilde{x}_{0}} q\left(x_{t-1}, x_{t} \mid \tilde{x}_{0}\right) \tilde{p}_{\theta}\left(\tilde{x}_{0} \mid x_{t}\right) \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ⊙ represents an element-wise product. For Equation 5 rules of conditional probability and Markov properties are used to define q(xt−1,xt∣x~0)\begin{document}$q\left(x_{t-1}, x_{t} \mid \tilde{x}_{0}\right)$\end{document} in terms of xt and x~0\begin{document}$\tilde{x}_{0}$\end{document}:
(6)
q(xx−1,xt∣x~0)=Cat(xx−1;p=xtQt⊙x~0Q¯t−1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} q\left(x_{x-1}, x_{t} \mid \tilde{x}_{0}\right)=\operatorname{Cat}\left(x_{x-1} ; p=x_{t} Q_{t} \odot \tilde{x}_{0} \bar{Q}_{t-1}\right) \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Putting everything together, at each step of training a corruption timestep is sampled according to t ∼ 𝒰 (1, …, T − 1). xt is then sampled via q(xt∣x0)∼Cat(xt;p=x0Qt)\begin{document}$q\left(x_{t} \mid x_{0}\right) \sim \operatorname{Cat}\left(x_{t} ; p=x_{0} Q_{t}\right)$\end{document} for every residue in the input protein, and the neural network predicts p~θ(x~0∣xt)\begin{document}$\tilde{p}_{\theta}\left(\tilde{x}_{0} \mid x_{t}\right)$\end{document}. Note that, while the corruption and loss are computed independently over each residue, the neural network predicts p\begin{document}$p$\end{document} in the context of the entire sequence. If t = 1, only the loss ℒ0 is used, reflecting a standard negative log likelihood. Otherwise, Equations 4 and 5 are used to compute the loss ℒt−1.

LRARs can be expanded into a diffusion framework via two subtle changes. Following the exposition in Hoogeboom et al., (24), the first change is to allow order-agnostic decoding. In an order-agnostic autoregressive model, a decoding order σ is first sampled uniformly from all possible decoding orders SL. At time step t in the forward process, xσ(L−t) is masked. The log-likelihood for an order-agnostic autoregressive model is derived using Jensen’s inequality:
log⁡p(x)=log⁡Eσ∼U(SL)p(x∣σ)≥Eσ∼U(SL)log⁡p(x∣σ)≥Eσ∼U(SL)∑t=1Llog⁡p(xσ(t)∣xσ(<t)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \log p(\boldsymbol{x})=\log \mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} p(\boldsymbol{x} \mid \sigma) & \geq \mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} \log p(\boldsymbol{x} \mid \sigma) \\ & \geq \mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} \sum_{t=1}^{L} \log p\left(x_{\sigma(t)} \mid \boldsymbol{x}_{\sigma(<t)}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
The next change involves an objective that optimizes over arbitrary decoding orders one timestep at a time in the style of modern diffusion models, without requiring a neural network that enforces a triangular or causal dependency structure. This is accomplished by replacing the summation over t by an expectation that is appropriately re-weighted.
log⁡p(x)≥Eσ∼U(SL)∑t=1Llog⁡p(xσ(t)∣xσ(<t))=Eσ∼U(SL)L⋅Et∼U(1,...,L)log⁡p(xσ(t)∣xσ(<t))=L⋅Et∼U(1,...,L)Eσ∼U(SL)1L−t+1∑k∈σ(≥t)log⁡p(xk∣xσ(<t)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \log p(\boldsymbol{x}) & \geq \mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} \sum_{t=1}^{L} \log p\left(x_{\sigma(t)} \mid \boldsymbol{x}_{\sigma(<t)}\right) \\ & =\mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} L \cdot \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(1, . . ., L)} \log p\left(x_{\sigma(t)} \mid \boldsymbol{x}_{\sigma(<t)}\right) \\ & =L \cdot \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(1, . . ., L)} \mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} \frac{1}{L-t+1} \sum_{k \in \sigma(\geq t)} \log p\left(x_{k} \mid \boldsymbol{x}_{\sigma(<t)}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
The overall expected log likelihood log p(x) can be thought of according to a series of likelihoods, each captured in the loss at step t, ℒt:
(8)
Lt=1L−t+1Eσ∼U(SL)∑k∈σ(≥t)log⁡p(xk∣xσ(<t)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}_{t}=\frac{1}{L-t+1} \mathbb{E}_{\sigma \sim \mathcal{U}\left(S_{L}\right)} \sum_{k \in \sigma(\geq t)} \log p\left(x_{k} \mid \boldsymbol{x}_{\sigma(<t)}\right) . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Thus, the overall expected log likelihood is lower bounded as:
(9)
log⁡p(x)≥Et∼U(1,…,L)[L⋅Lt] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \log p(\boldsymbol{x}) \geq \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}(1, \ldots, L)}\left[L \cdot \mathcal{L}_{t}\right] \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
A neural network can be efficiently trained to learn the reverse process pθ(xσ(t)|xσ(<t)) by randomly masking a set of t tokens at each iteration and minimizing the reweighted loss, allowing the model to learn from predictions of all masked positions at each timestep. By learning one model over all possible decoding orders, OADM allows for conditioning by fixing arbitrary subsequences at generation time. Sequences were generated unconditionally from OADM models by beginning with an all-mask sequence as input, randomly sampling a decoding order, and sampling each token from the predicted probability distribution.
